{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-52254",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n5{h}^{2}-12h-9.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($5\\times -9=-45$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-12$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-45$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-45),\\,\\,(-1,45),\\,\\,(3,-15),\\,\\,(-3,15),\\,\\,(5,-9),\\,\\,(-5,9).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(3,-15)$ add to $-12:$ \n\n$$\n3-15=-12\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $3h-15h,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{h}^{2}-12h-9 & = \\\\ 5{h}^{2}+3h-15h-9 & = \\\\ 5{h}^{2}-15h+3h-9 & = \\\\ (5{h}^{2}-15h)+(3h-9) & = \\\\ 5h(h-3)+3(h-3) & = \\\\ (5h+3)(h-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
